WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.120
Speaker 1: All right. I want to start today with&nbsp;
a little data, some numbers. One often repeated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:11.760
claim about gun violence in the United States&nbsp;
is the gun violence is a problem in so-called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:18.800
Democrat cities with the strictest gun laws in the&nbsp;
country. And there are sort of two claims that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:25.600
right loves to make that are implicit in that one.&nbsp;
The problems with gun violence are in places that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:33.200
try to restrict guns, meaning restrictive gun laws&nbsp;
increase violence and murder. And the second claim&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:40.160
is the most violent places are the places run by&nbsp;
Democrats. I put together data in a few different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:45.200
ways and we're going to look at it. But the most&nbsp;
important I mean, before even looking at the data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:50.320
one of the things you have to remember is that&nbsp;
in general, there is more crime in more densely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:57.920
populated areas. The more densely populated areas&nbsp;
tend to be places where people on the left tend to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:05.200
live. But the truth is that the more violent&nbsp;
cities and the least violent cities are all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:11.520
mostly run by Democrats because Democrats prefer&nbsp;
to live in cities, whereas Republicans prefer to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:17.200
live in rural areas, generally speaking. So one&nbsp;
of the things you first have to think about is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:25.840
what does the country look like before we even&nbsp;
can make any causal claims? But that being said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:33.920
let's take a look at some of this data. So what&nbsp;
I did was I took the where am I here? I took the.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:42.160
Here we go. I took the most the 65 cities with the&nbsp;
highest murder rates in the country, and that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:47.440
along the left. And you can find this data.&nbsp;
And those are the cities that I have listed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:53.200
And then we looked at who is the mayor of those&nbsp;
cities and are they a Democrat or a Republican?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:59.280
And then at the county level, was that a&nbsp;
Biden county or a Trump County in 2020?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:05.120
And then at the state level, was that a Trump&nbsp;
state or a Biden state in 2020? Now, I have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:10.960
tell you, even doing this is a little fraught&nbsp;
because there are certain states, Virginia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:17.600
a bunch of these cities are in Virginia in 2020,&nbsp;
Biden won, but sometimes Republicans win Virginia.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:22.800
So some of these states are question marks. Ohio&nbsp;
is a state that sometimes flips. Florida is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:29.280
state that sometimes slips. But there is a trend&nbsp;
here that is very clear. The cities with the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:36.400
the highest murder rates at the city level, it's&nbsp;
mostly Democratic mayors. Now, we would expect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:41.760
that that makes perfect sense. Cities tend to be&nbsp;
run by Democrats. If we looked at a list of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:47.040
cities with the least gun violence, you would also&nbsp;
see that they're mostly run by Democrats. Cities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:53.840
often make up the entire county in which they&nbsp;
are or close to it. In New York City, actually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:58.720
there's the city includes multiple counties,&nbsp;
Brooklyn and Kings County, whereas Manhattan.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:02.880
Okay. Some people sort of understand that. But&nbsp;
generally speaking, you would assume that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:10.000
county would be similar to the city in that cities&nbsp;
and counties often are the same size or very close&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:15.840
to it. And most of the counties that contain these&nbsp;
cities were Biden counties. Well, is that a big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:21.920
revelation about gun violence? Not really. Because&nbsp;
if you look at the entire map of the United States&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:28.080
by county, we know that cities tend to vote for&nbsp;
Democrats and all the other counties tend to vote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:33.760
for Republicans typically. So cities on the most&nbsp;
dangerous list and cities on the least dangerous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:40.960
list would all be on the add on would be run by&nbsp;
would be Democratic counties. And then lastly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:43.840
you look at the state level and&nbsp;
when you look at the state level,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:50.240
you see that there's a lot of red there. A lot,&nbsp;
a lot, a lot of red. Trump states that Trump won.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:58.160
And when we want to draw a causal relationship,&nbsp;
one of the questions we have to ask is whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:06.640
gun policy tends to be set at the city, county or&nbsp;
state level, and it's mostly at the state level.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:13.840
So if we understand most of the murders will&nbsp;
be where there is more population density,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:18.960
but the gun laws are mostly going&nbsp;
to be set at the state level.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:26.560
You have to ask, well, does Saint Louis have a&nbsp;
lot of murders because the mayor is a Democrat?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:34.560
Or because it's a red state with red state gun&nbsp;
laws. I'm not pretending that the answer in all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:42.800
cases is one or the other. But if we understand&nbsp;
that it's mostly states that determine gun policy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:48.080
although there are some cities that will go&nbsp;
above and beyond, that's absolutely the case.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:55.040
And we understand that at the base rate, Democrats&nbsp;
tend to live more in cities than Republicans.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.040 --> 00:05:00.880
And we understand that even if you're in Chicago,&nbsp;
Chicago's gun laws, if you can drive into Indiana&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:06.160
at the end of the day, don't really impact your&nbsp;
ability to get a gun very much unless there's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:14.000
cohesive effort. This repeated talking point of&nbsp;
gun violence is a Democrat city problem. Very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:20.720
quickly falls apart. Now I am totally open to&nbsp;
exploring other explanations. Let let me give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:27.360
you another one. We can also accurately say&nbsp;
gun violence seems to be a red state problem.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:34.960
But there as well, I'm willing to say maybe access&nbsp;
to mental health is part of it. We've got to deal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:39.680
with the guns, but maybe mental health is&nbsp;
part of it. But it's the red states that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:45.120
fund mental health services less than blue&nbsp;
states. We looked at that earlier this week.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:48.160
It's the red states like Texas that&nbsp;
have governors like Greg Abbott,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:53.840
who recently voted against more funding for mental&nbsp;
health treatment. So I say let's look at all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.840 --> 00:06:00.320
these things, but we have to include guns in that.&nbsp;
And we have to understand Democrat city problem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:07.040
is not accurate and there's no particular reason&nbsp;
to look at cities rather than states. When you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.040 --> 00:06:13.360
look at states, it's a red state problem. If you&nbsp;
look at cities, it's a Democrat problem only in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:17.680
the sense that most cities are run by Democrats,&nbsp;
the really safe ones and the unsafe ones. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:20.560
I think you get the picture. We're&nbsp;
going to continue following up on this.

